#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124296 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "97730a2e-3d3e-5a2c-b366-7a3b1eafbe63" :knowledge-point/title "Reconstructing a Two-Variable Function From Its Gradient"}] :question/id #uuid "ff1853b3-627e-5c73-be98-b1d9a586f198" :question/math-academy-id "e-7020" :question/problem "Find $f(x,y)$ such that $\\nabla f = \\mathbf{F}$, where $\\mathbf F(x,y) = 2xy\\ \\mathbf i + (x^2 - 3)\\ \\mathbf j$." :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the function $f(x,y)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y) = \\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{x}}\\,\\textbf{i} + \\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{y}}\\,\\textbf{j}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y) = \\mathbf F(x,y)\n$$\n\nthe function $f(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{x}} = 2xy, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{y}} = x^2-3\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{x}}\n$$\n\nwith respect to $x$. Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x, y) &= ∫\\frac{∂f}{∂x}dx \\\\\n&= ∫2xydx \\\\\n&= x^{2}y + g(y)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{y}} = x^2 + g'(y)\n$$\n\nTaking\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{y}} = x^2 -3\n$$\n\ninto account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} + g^{′}(y) &= x^{2} - 3 \\\\\ng^{′}(y) &= -3 \\\\\ng(y) &= -3y + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nFinally, substituting\n\n$$\ng(y)=-3y +C\n$$\n\ninto our expression for $f(x,y)$, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = x^2y -3y + C\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124300 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "90458c6e-3d72-5235-a1c0-627fe4fd2855" :knowledge-point/title "Reconstructing a Three-Variable Function From Its Gradient"}] :question/id #uuid "09d12c46-4ac8-53d9-80a6-ca68f98e0c5a" :question/math-academy-id "e-7184" :question/problem "Find $f(x,y,z)$ such that $\\nabla f(x,y,z) = \\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)$, where $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z) = yz\\,\\mathbf{i} + (xz + 4z^2)\\,\\mathbf{j} + (xy+8yz)\\,\\mathbf{k}$." :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the function $f(x,y,z)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y,z) = \\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{x}} \\, \\mathbf{i} + \\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{y}} \\, \\mathbf{j} + \\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{z}} \\, \\mathbf{k}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y,z) = \\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)\n$$\n\nthe function $f(x,y,z)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{x}} = yz, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{y}} = xz + 4z^2, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{z}} = xy+8yz\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{x}}\n$$\n\nwith respect to $x$. Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y$ and $z$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x, y, z) &= ∫\\frac{∂f}{∂x}dx \\\\\n&= ∫yzdx \\\\\n&= xyz + g(y, z)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{y}} = xz + \\dfrac{\\partial{g}}{\\partial{y}}\n$$\n\nTaking\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{y}} = xz + 4z^2\n$$\n\ninto account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nxz + \\frac{∂g}{∂y} &= xz + 4z^{2} \\\\\n\\frac{∂g}{∂y} &= 4z^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial{g}}{\\partial{y}}\n$$\n\nwith respect to $y$. Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $z$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng(y, z) &= ∫\\frac{∂g}{∂y}dy \\\\\n&= ∫4z^{2}dy \\\\\n&= 4yz^{2} + h(z)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\ng(y,z) = 4yz^2 + h(z)\n$$\n\nin the expression for $f(x, y,z)$, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z) = xyz + 4yz^2 + h(z)\n$$\n\nTo determine the function $h(z)$, we differentiate $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $z$. This gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{z}} = xy + 8yz + h'(z)\n$$\n\nTaking\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{z}} = xy+8yz\n$$\n\ninto account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nxy + 8yz &= xy + 8yz + h^{′}(z) \\\\\nh^{′}(z) &= 0 \\\\\nh(z) &= C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z) = xyz + 4yz^2 + C\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124859 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "97730a2e-3d3e-5a2c-b366-7a3b1eafbe63" :knowledge-point/title "Reconstructing a Two-Variable Function From Its Gradient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83e12610-8006-5ba7-b873-f5242112b8c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x, y) =-xy + 3y + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122177} {:answer/id #uuid "aa50ddea-b894-5bdf-87b1-5cacd3fde362" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x, y) =-xy - y + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122178} {:answer/id #uuid "b01e9ed0-e793-562e-8a37-34a080cb2278" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x, y) = 3 - xy + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122179} {:answer/id #uuid "eee6c100-4c1f-5783-8f2f-139827ad8df1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x, y) =-xy + 2x + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122180} {:answer/id #uuid "fa695782-0c80-500f-908d-74f8d7815366" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x, y) = \\frac{yx^{2}}{2} + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122181}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "83e12610-8006-5ba7-b873-f5242112b8c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x, y) =-xy + 3y + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122177} :answer-field/id #uuid "d9b2c99c-9889-5c64-81eb-b38fc186f43f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122176}] :question/id #uuid "c542ab02-5931-58f2-9193-271289d6b23c" :question/math-academy-id "q-35994" :question/problem "Find $f(x, y)$ such that $∇f = F$, where $F(x, y) =-yi + (3 - x)j$."}]}
